<h2 id="origin">1. Where innate cells come from</h2>
<p>All blood cells come from one haematopoietic stem cell (HSC) in the bone marrow. This includes granulocytes, monocytes, dendritic cells (DCs), mast cells, lymphocytes and innate lymphoid cells (ILCs). <span class="src" title="M2-F001">Abbas p.30</span> HSCs express CD34 and c-KIT and do not express lineage markers. <span class="src" title="M2-F002">Abbas p.30</span></p>
<p>Two branches follow:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Myeloid branch.</b> Common myeloid-megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors make the granulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and monocytes. <span class="src" title="M2-F003">Abbas p.32</span> Most DCs come from a precursor that also makes monocytes. <span class="src" title="M2-F004">Abbas p.32</span> Mast cell progenitors come from a granulocyte/monocyte precursor, leave the marrow, and mature in the tissues. <span class="src" title="M2-F005">Abbas p.32</span> Basophils mature in the marrow from progenitors that are different from mast cell progenitors. <span class="src" title="M2-F010">Abbas p.20</span></li>
  <li><b>Lymphoid branch.</b> The common lymphoid progenitor makes T cells, B cells and a shared precursor of natural killer (NK) cells and ILCs. <span class="src" title="M2-F006">Abbas p.30</span></li>
</ul>
<p>Key growth factors: G-CSF and GM-CSF drive neutrophil production <span class="src" title="M2-F007">Abbas p.14</span>; M-CSF drives the monocyte-macrophage lineage <span class="src" title="M2-F008">Abbas p.15</span>; all DC subsets need FLT3 ligand (FLT3L) and express CD11c. <span class="src" title="M2-F009">Abbas p.20</span> In normal blood, neutrophils are 40–60%, eosinophils 1–4%, basophils &lt;1% and monocytes 2–8% of leukocytes. <span class="src" title="M2-F012">Abbas p.14</span></p>

