<div class="box caution"><span class="label">Source difference</span>
<p>Abbas (11th ed.) states that most DCs come from a myeloid precursor shared with monocytes. <span class="src" title="M2-F004">Abbas p.32</span> Janeway (8th ed.) states that DCs come from both myeloid and lymphoid progenitors. <span class="src" title="M2-F011">Janeway p.77</span> Janeway also describes tissue macrophages as cells that mature continuously from monocytes. <span class="src" title="M2-F024">Janeway p.76</span> Abbas (newer) states that most tissue-resident macrophages come from yolk sac or fetal liver precursors. <span class="src" title="M2-F022">Abbas p.15</span> Use the Abbas version for the exam.</p></div>

<h2 id="neutrophils">2. Neutrophils</h2>
<p>Neutrophils are the most abundant circulating white blood cells and the principal cell of acute inflammation. <span class="src" title="M2-F013">Abbas p.14</span> The nucleus has three to five lobes. For this reason, neutrophils are also called polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMNs). <span class="src" title="M2-F014">Abbas p.14</span></p>
<p>Neutrophils have two types of granules:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Specific granules</b> (the majority): lysozyme, collagenase and elastase. <span class="src" title="M2-F015">Abbas p.14</span></li>
  <li><b>Azurophilic granules</b>: myeloperoxidase (MPO), defensins and cathelicidins. <span class="src" title="M2-F016">Abbas p.14</span></li>
</ul>
<p>Human neutrophil markers include CD11b, CD18, CD15, CD66b and CD10. <span class="src" title="M2-F017">Abbas p.15</span> Neutrophils live only 1–2 days in tissues. <span class="src" title="M2-F018">Abbas p.15</span> They respond fast because they activate preformed enzymes. Macrophages respond more slowly, and often need new gene transcription. <span class="src" title="M2-F019">Abbas p.15</span> Neutrophils can also extrude their nuclear contents as neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs). NETs immobilize and kill extracellular microbes, but they can also damage healthy tissue. <span class="src" title="M2-F020">Abbas p.15</span></p>
<div class="box clinical"><span class="label">Clinical correlation</span>
<p>Defects of neutrophil killing and migration cause recurrent bacterial and fungal infections. Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is caused by defective phagocyte oxidase and reactive oxygen species. <span class="src" title="M2-F105">Abbas p.487</span> Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 (LAD-1) is caused by mutations in CD18, the β chain of β2 integrins. <span class="src" title="M2-F106">Abbas p.487</span> Module M4 covers these defects in detail.</p></div>

<h2 id="macrophages">3. Monocytes and macrophages</h2>
<h3>Two origins</h3>
<p>Classical (inflammatory) monocytes make up 90–95% of human blood monocytes. They are rapidly recruited to sites of infection or injury, where they become macrophages. <span class="src" title="M2-F021">Abbas p.15-16</span> Most tissue-resident macrophages have a different origin. They come from yolk sac or fetal liver precursors, live for a long time and renew themselves. <span class="src" title="M2-F022">Abbas p.15</span> Examples are Kupffer cells (liver), alveolar macrophages (lung) and microglia (brain). <span class="src" title="M2-F023">Abbas p.15</span> Human macrophage markers include CD11b, CD14, CD68, CD16, CD64 and CCR5. <span class="src" title="M2-F025">Abbas p.15</span></p>
<h3>Functions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Cytokine production: activated macrophages make TNF, IL-1, IL-6 and IL-12. <span class="src" title="M2-F032">Abbas p.19</span> (Phagocytosis and killing: see M4.)</li>
  <li>Removal of apoptotic cells before they release their contents (efferocytosis). <span class="src" title="M2-F026">Abbas p.16</span></li>
  <li>Protection of healthy cells: the inhibitory receptor SIRPα binds CD47, a "don't eat me" signal on healthy cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F027">Abbas p.19</span></li>
</ul>
<h3>Classical (M1) versus alternative (M2) activation</h3>
<p>Microbial products such as lipopolysaccharide and NK cell-derived interferon-γ (IFN-γ) activate macrophages. <span class="src" title="M2-F029">Abbas p.19</span> Classical activation gives microbicidal M1 macrophages. Alternative activation gives M2 macrophages, which promote tissue remodeling and repair. <span class="src" title="M2-F028">Abbas p.19</span> In Abbas Fig. 2.4, cytokines such as IL-4 and IL-13 drive collagen and angiogenic-factor production, which leads to repair and fibrosis. <span class="src" title="M2-F030">Abbas p.19</span> Many macrophage subsets do not fit neatly into M1 or M2. <span class="src" title="M2-F031">Abbas p.19</span></p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>M1 (classical)</th><th>M2 (alternative)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Typical stimuli</td><td>IFN-γ, microbial products (e.g., lipopolysaccharide) <span class="src" title="M2-F029">Abbas p.19</span></td><td>IL-4, IL-13 (Abbas Fig. 2.4) <span class="src" title="M2-F030">Abbas p.19</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Main job</td><td>Kill microbes; inflammation <span class="src" title="M2-F028">Abbas p.19</span></td><td>Tissue remodeling, repair, fibrosis <span class="src" title="M2-F028,M2-F030">Abbas p.19</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="box analogy"><span class="label">Analogy</span>
<p>An M1 macrophage is a soldier that destroys invaders. An M2 macrophage is a builder that repairs the damage after the fight.</p></div>
<p>Not covered in provided sources — needs expert input: specific surface markers used to tell M1 from M2 macrophages in humans (Abbas refers to Chapter 10, which is not in the source set).</p>

<h2 id="dcs">4. Dendritic cells: the bridge to adaptive immunity</h2>
<p>DCs are sentinels in epithelia and tissues. They capture antigens and carry them to lymph nodes, where naive T cells circulate. In this way they link innate and adaptive immunity. <span class="src" title="M2-F033">Abbas p.20</span> DCs activated by pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) express costimulators. They also direct naive T cells to become effector subsets such as Th1 or Th17 cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F041">Abbas p.81</span></p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Subset</th><th>Key features</th><th>Main role</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>cDC2</td><td>Most numerous DC <span class="src" title="M2-F034">Abbas p.21</span></td><td>Presents antigen mainly to CD4+ T cells <span class="src" title="M2-F034">Abbas p.21</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>cDC1</td><td>IRF8, BATF3; CD141, CLEC9A, XCR1; makes IL-12 <span class="src" title="M2-F035">Abbas p.21-23</span></td><td>Cross-presents antigen to naive CD8+ T cells; induces Th1 responses <span class="src" title="M2-F035">Abbas p.21-23</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Plasmacytoid DC (pDC)</td><td>BDCA-2 (CD303), CD123; high TLR7 and TLR9; transcription factor E2-2 <span class="src" title="M2-F036">Abbas p.23</span></td><td>Main source of antiviral type I IFN <span class="src" title="M2-F037">Abbas p.22</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Langerhans cell</td><td>Epidermis; Birbeck granules; langerin (CD207); embryonic origin like tissue macrophages <span class="src" title="M2-F039">Abbas p.22</span></td><td>Antigen presentation to CD4+ T cells or tolerance <span class="src" title="M2-F039">Abbas p.22</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>pDCs can make up to 1000 times more type I IFN than other cell types. <span class="src" title="M2-F038">Janeway p.111</span></p>
<div class="box pearl"><span class="label">Exam pearl</span>
<p>Follicular dendritic cells are NOT dendritic cells in the immunological sense. They are stromal cells, they do not come from bone marrow, and they do not present protein antigens to T cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F040">Abbas p.22</span></p></div>

<h2 id="nk">5. Natural killer (NK) cells</h2>
<h3>Identity</h3>
<p>NK cells are 5–20% of lymphocytes in blood and spleen. They are abundant in liver and placenta. <span class="src" title="M2-F042">Abbas p.82</span> In blood they are CD56-positive and CD3-negative, and most express CD16. <span class="src" title="M2-F043">Abbas p.82</span> NK cells kill without prior activation and use germline-encoded receptors, not clonal antigen receptors. <span class="src" title="M2-F044">Abbas p.82</span></p>
<h3>Two effector functions</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li>The NK cell kills an infected or stressed cell. Perforin helps granzymes enter the target cell. Granzymes then trigger apoptosis. <span class="src" title="M2-F046">Abbas p.82</span></li>
  <li>The NK cell secretes IFN-γ. IFN-γ activates macrophages to destroy the microbes that they have phagocytosed. <span class="src" title="M2-F045">Abbas p.82</span></li>
</ol>
<p>NK cells act early. They kill virus-infected cells before antigen-specific cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs) become fully active after 5–7 days. <span class="src" title="M2-F047">Abbas p.82</span> IL-12, IL-15, IL-18 and type I IFNs increase NK killing and IFN-γ secretion. IL-15 is also an NK growth factor. <span class="src" title="M2-F048">Abbas p.85</span></p>

<h3>The balance: activating versus inhibitory receptors</h3>
<p>Each NK cell adds up signals from activating and inhibitory receptors. The balance decides the outcome. <span class="src" title="M2-F049">Abbas p.83</span></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Inhibitory receptors</b> recognise MHC class I (MHC-I). Inhibitory KIRs bind classical MHC-I molecules. The CD94/NKG2A heterodimer binds HLA-E, which displays peptides from other MHC-I molecules. <span class="src" title="M2-F054">Abbas p.85</span> Inhibitory receptors carry ITIMs, which recruit phosphatases that block activating signals. <span class="src" title="M2-F055">Abbas p.85</span></li>
  <li><b>Activating receptors</b> signal through ITAMs. <span class="src" title="M2-F055">Abbas p.85</span> NKG2D binds the stress proteins MIC-A, MIC-B and ULBP on infected and tumour cells, and signals through DAP10. <span class="src" title="M2-F050">Abbas p.83</span> The natural cytotoxicity receptors (NCRs) NKp30, NKp44 and NKp46 are also activating. <span class="src" title="M2-F051">Janeway p.117</span></li>
  <li><b>Same family, opposite signals.</b> Inhibitory KIRs (KIR-2DL, KIR-3DL) have long tails with ITIMs. Activating KIRs (KIR-2DS, KIR-3DS) have short tails and pair with DAP12. <span class="src" title="M2-F056">Janeway p.115-116</span> NKG2A is inhibitory, but NKG2C pairs with DAP12 and is activating. <span class="src" title="M2-F057">Janeway p.116</span></li>
</ul>
<p><b>Missing self.</b> Viruses, cell stress and many cancers reduce MHC-I on the cell surface. The NK cell then loses its inhibitory signal and is activated. This is called recognition of missing self. <span class="src" title="M2-F053">Abbas p.85</span> Interferons increase MHC-I on uninfected cells and so protect them from NK cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F058">Janeway p.115</span></p>

<figure class="diagram" id="D6">
<svg viewBox="0 0 800 550" role="img" aria-labelledby="D6t">
<title id="D6t">NK cell balance: a healthy cell with MHC-I inhibits the NK cell; an infected cell that lost MHC-I and expresses stress ligands activates killing</title>
<text x="400" y="26" class="t-title" text-anchor="middle">NK cell decision: a balance of signals</text>
<!-- Panel A -->
<rect x="10" y="44" width="380" height="262" rx="8" fill="#f5f7fa" stroke="#d9dee7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="24" y="68" class="t-head">A · Healthy cell → no killing</text>
<rect x="24" y="84" width="96" height="150" rx="8" fill="#ece5f5" stroke="#6a4c93" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="72" y="108" class="t-head" text-anchor="middle">NK cell</text>
<text x="72" y="200" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">ITIM →</text>
<text x="72" y="214" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">phosphatase</text>
<rect x="280" y="84" width="96" height="150" rx="8" fill="#e3f1ea" stroke="#2e7d5b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="328" y="108" class="t-head" text-anchor="middle">Healthy</text>
<text x="328" y="136" class="t-body" text-anchor="middle">MHC-I</text>
<text x="328" y="152" class="t-body" text-anchor="middle">present</text>
<text x="328" y="190" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">no stress</text>
<text x="328" y="204" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">ligands</text>
<line x1="120" y1="135" x2="280" y2="135" stroke="#b23a48" stroke-width="4"/>
<text x="200" y="126" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">KIR · CD94/NKG2A</text>
<text x="200" y="152" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">bind MHC-I / HLA-E</text>
<line x1="120" y1="190" x2="280" y2="190" stroke="#2e7d5b" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>
<text x="200" y="182" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">NKG2D: no ligand</text>
<rect x="24" y="248" width="352" height="46" rx="8" fill="#f7e1e4" stroke="#b23a48" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="200" y="267" class="t-body" text-anchor="middle">Inhibitory signal wins</text>
<text x="200" y="285" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">NK cell spares the healthy cell</text>
<!-- Panel B -->
<rect x="410" y="44" width="380" height="262" rx="8" fill="#f5f7fa" stroke="#d9dee7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="424" y="68" class="t-head">B · Infected or tumour cell → killing</text>
<rect x="424" y="84" width="96" height="150" rx="8" fill="#ece5f5" stroke="#6a4c93" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="472" y="108" class="t-head" text-anchor="middle">NK cell</text>
<text x="472" y="200" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">perforin +</text>
<text x="472" y="214" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">granzymes</text>
<rect x="680" y="84" width="96" height="150" rx="8" fill="#f7e1e4" stroke="#b23a48" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="728" y="108" class="t-head" text-anchor="middle">Infected</text>
<text x="728" y="136" class="t-body" text-anchor="middle">MHC-I</text>
<text x="728" y="152" class="t-body" text-anchor="middle">lost</text>
<text x="728" y="190" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">MIC-A/B,</text>
<text x="728" y="204" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">ULBP ↑</text>
<line x1="520" y1="135" x2="680" y2="135" stroke="#5b6475" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>
<text x="600" y="126" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">KIR: nothing to bind</text>
<text x="600" y="152" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">= missing self</text>
<line x1="520" y1="190" x2="680" y2="190" stroke="#2e7d5b" stroke-width="4"/>
<text x="600" y="182" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">NKG2D binds ligand</text>
<rect x="424" y="248" width="352" height="46" rx="8" fill="#e3f1ea" stroke="#2e7d5b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="600" y="267" class="t-body" text-anchor="middle">Activating signal wins</text>
<text x="600" y="285" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">perforin + granzymes → apoptosis of target</text>
<!-- Receptor lists -->
<rect x="10" y="322" width="380" height="186" rx="8" fill="#e3f1ea" stroke="#2e7d5b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="24" y="346" class="t-head" style="fill:#2e7d5b">Activating receptors (ITAM or DAP10)</text>
<text x="24" y="374" class="t-body">NKG2D (DAP10) → MIC-A, MIC-B, ULBP</text>
<text x="24" y="398" class="t-body">NCRs: NKp30, NKp44, NKp46</text>
<text x="24" y="422" class="t-body">KIR-2DS / KIR-3DS: short tail, DAP12</text>
<text x="24" y="446" class="t-body">CD94/NKG2C (DAP12)</text>
<text x="24" y="470" class="t-body">CD16 (FcγRIIIA) → IgG1, IgG3 = ADCC</text>
<rect x="410" y="322" width="380" height="186" rx="8" fill="#f7e1e4" stroke="#b23a48" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<text x="424" y="346" class="t-head" style="fill:#b23a48">Inhibitory receptors (ITIM)</text>
<text x="424" y="374" class="t-body">KIR-2DL / KIR-3DL: long tail, ITIM</text>
<text x="424" y="398" class="t-body">→ classical MHC-I molecules</text>
<text x="424" y="422" class="t-body">CD94/NKG2A → HLA-E</text>
<text x="424" y="446" class="t-body">HLA-E reports overall MHC-I</text>
<text x="424" y="470" class="t-body">ITIM → phosphatases block signals</text>
<text x="424" y="494" class="t-body">IFN raises MHC-I → protects healthy cells</text>
<text x="400" y="534" class="t-small" text-anchor="middle">IL-12, IL-15, IL-18 and type I IFN increase NK killing and IFN-γ release.</text>
</svg>
<figcaption><b>D6.</b> NK cell "missing self". The NK cell integrates activating and inhibitory signals. Loss of MHC-I removes inhibition; stress ligands add activation. CD16 lets NK cells kill IgG-coated cells (ADCC). Fact IDs M2-F049, M2-F054, M2-F055, M2-F050, M2-F051, M2-F056, M2-F057, M2-F052, M2-F053, M2-F058, M2-F046, M2-F048. <span class="src">Abbas p.83</span> <span class="src">Abbas p.85</span> <span class="src">Janeway p.115-116</span> <span class="src">Janeway p.117</span></figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>CD16 and ADCC</h3>
<p>CD16 (FcγRIIIA) is a low-affinity receptor for IgG. It binds IgG1 and IgG3 on antibody-coated cells. The NK cell then kills the coated cell. This is antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC). <span class="src" title="M2-F052">Abbas p.84</span></p>
<div class="box clinical"><span class="label">Clinical correlation: NK cell deficiency</span>
<p>Patients with numerical or functional NK cell deficiency have severe herpesvirus and papillomavirus infections. <span class="src" title="M2-F059">Abbas p.488</span> Autosomal dominant GATA2 mutations cause loss of NK cells, together with low monocytes, DCs and B cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F060">Abbas p.488</span> Autosomal recessive CD16 mutations cause a functional NK cell deficiency. <span class="src" title="M2-F061">Abbas p.488</span> In Chédiak-Higashi syndrome, NK function is poor because the cytotoxic granules are abnormal. <span class="src" title="M2-F062">Abbas p.488</span></p></div>

<h2 id="granulocytes">6. Mast cells, basophils and eosinophils</h2>
<p>These three myeloid cells all have granules full of inflammatory mediators. All three take part in defence against helminths and in allergic disease. <span class="src" title="M2-F107">Abbas p.20</span> They differ in location, life span, receptors and mediators. <span class="src" title="M2-F065,M2-F067">Abbas p.466</span></p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Mast cell</th><th>Basophil</th><th>Eosinophil</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Where</td><td>Tissues; near vessels, nerves, under epithelia; not in blood <span class="src" title="M2-F063">Abbas p.465</span></td><td>Blood (0.5% or less of leukocytes); recruited to tissues <span class="src" title="M2-F080">Abbas p.465</span></td><td>Blood; normally also in respiratory, gut and genitourinary mucosa <span class="src" title="M2-F084">Abbas p.20</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Life span <span class="src" title="M2-F065">Abbas p.466</span></td><td>Weeks to months</td><td>Days</td><td>Days to weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main growth factor <span class="src" title="M2-F066">Abbas p.466</span></td><td>SCF, IL-3</td><td>IL-3</td><td>IL-5</td></tr>
<tr><td>FcεRI <span class="src" title="M2-F067">Abbas p.466</span></td><td>High (αβγ2)</td><td>High (αβγ2)</td><td>Low (αγ2), no proven IgE activation <span class="src" title="M2-F069,M2-F090">Abbas p.467; Abbas p.473</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Main granule contents</td><td colspan="2">Histamine; heparin and/or chondroitin sulfate; neutral proteases (tryptase and/or chymase) <span class="src" title="M2-F072">Abbas p.467</span></td><td>MBP, ECP, eosinophil peroxidase <span class="src" title="M2-F085,M2-F086">Abbas p.473</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>New mediators</td><td colspan="2">PGD2, leukotrienes C4/D4/E4, PAF; cytokines <span class="src" title="M2-F073">Abbas p.467</span></td><td>Galectin-10 (Charcot-Leyden crystals) <span class="src" title="M2-F087">Abbas p.473</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h3>Mast cells</h3>
<p>Stem cell factor (SCF) binds c-KIT on mast cell precursors and is essential for mast cell development. <span class="src" title="M2-F064">Abbas p.465</span> FcεRI binds IgE with very high affinity. As a result, most mast cells carry IgE even in nonatopic people. <span class="src" title="M2-F068">Abbas p.466</span> On mast cells and basophils, FcεRI is an αβγ2 tetramer. <span class="src" title="M2-F069">Abbas p.467</span></p>
<p>IgE-mediated activation follows this sequence:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li>Multivalent allergen binds IgE that sits on FcεRI. <span class="src" title="M2-F070">Abbas p.468</span></li>
  <li>The allergen cross-links the FcεRI molecules. <span class="src" title="M2-F070">Abbas p.468</span></li>
  <li>The mast cell releases preformed granule contents (degranulation). <span class="src" title="M2-F071">Abbas p.468</span></li>
  <li>The mast cell makes new lipid mediators and cytokines. <span class="src" title="M2-F071">Abbas p.468</span></li>
</ol>
<p>Mast cells can also be activated without IgE. Complement fragments C3a and C5a (the anaphylatoxins) cause degranulation. TLR ligands, ATP and cytokines such as SCF and IL-33 also activate mast cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F074">Abbas p.470</span> The receptor MRGPRX2 on skin mast cells binds some antibiotics, anaesthetic drugs and venom components. It is a likely mediator of some allergy-like drug reactions. <span class="src" title="M2-F075">Abbas p.470</span></p>
<div class="box clinical"><span class="label">Clinical correlation: tryptase and mastocytosis</span>
<p>Tryptase is present in all human mast cells and in no other known cell type. For this reason, serum tryptase is a marker of mast cell activation and is used to diagnose anaphylaxis. <span class="src" title="M2-F077">Abbas p.471</span> Human mast cell subsets contain tryptase only, or tryptase and chymase. <span class="src" title="M2-F078">Abbas p.465</span> Systemic mastocytosis (SM) is a clonal mast cell disorder associated with c-KIT mutations and increased mediator release, including tryptase. <span class="src" title="M2-F079">Abbas p.470-471</span></p></div>

<h3>Basophils</h3>
<p>Basophils are blood granulocytes. They are normally absent from tissues but can be recruited to inflammatory sites. <span class="src" title="M2-F080">Abbas p.465</span> Like mast cells, basophils express FcεRI, bind IgE and are triggered when antigen binds the IgE. <span class="src" title="M2-F081">Abbas p.465</span></p>

<h3>Eosinophils</h3>
<p>GM-CSF, IL-3 and IL-5 promote eosinophil maturation from myeloid precursors. <span class="src" title="M2-F082">Abbas p.20</span> Eosinophils carry Fc receptors for IgA and IgG, TLRs and IL-5 receptors. <span class="src" title="M2-F083">Abbas p.20</span> IL-5 from Th2 cells and ILC2s increases eosinophil production and activates mature eosinophils. <span class="src" title="M2-F089">Abbas p.473</span> Eotaxin (CCL11) binds CCR3 on eosinophils and recruits them into tissue. <span class="src" title="M2-F088">Abbas p.473</span></p>
<p>Major basic protein (MBP) and eosinophil cationic protein (ECP) damage helminths, bacteria and host cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F085">Abbas p.473</span> Eosinophil peroxidase is different from neutrophil MPO. It makes hypochlorous or hypobromous acid. <span class="src" title="M2-F086">Abbas p.473</span> Eosinophils make large amounts of galectin-10. Galectin-10 forms Charcot-Leyden crystals at sites of chronic type 2 inflammation, such as asthmatic bronchi. <span class="src" title="M2-F087">Abbas p.473</span></p>
<p>In the late-phase reaction, neutrophils, Th2 cells and eosinophils enter the tissue a few hours to 2 days after allergen exposure. <span class="src" title="M2-F091">Abbas p.465</span></p>
<div class="box pearl"><span class="label">Exam pearl</span>
<p>Eosinophils have very little FcεRI, and their receptor lacks the signalling β chain. There is no convincing evidence that allergen bound to IgE activates eosinophils. <span class="src" title="M2-F069,M2-F090">Abbas p.467; Abbas p.473</span> The cells that respond directly to IgE cross-linking are mast cells and basophils. <span class="src" title="M2-F067,M2-F081">Abbas p.466; Abbas p.465</span></p></div>
<p>Not covered in provided sources — needs expert input: basophil activation markers used in the basophil activation test, and eosinophil surface markers other than CCR3 and IL-5 receptor.</p>

<h2 id="ilc">7. Innate lymphoid cells (ILCs)</h2>
<p>ILCs are lymphoid cells without T-cell receptors. They secrete cytokines like those of helper T cells. They live mainly in mucosal tissues such as lung and intestine, and are rare in blood. <span class="src" title="M2-F092">Abbas p.30</span> A common ILC precursor is identified by the transcription factor Id2. <span class="src" title="M2-F093">Abbas p.81</span></p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Subset</th><th>Transcription factor <span class="src" title="M2-F094">Abbas p.81</span></th><th>Activated by <span class="src" title="M2-F095">Abbas p.81</span></th><th>Main cytokines <span class="src" title="M2-F094">Abbas p.81</span></th><th>Function <span class="src" title="M2-F096">Abbas p.81-82</span></th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>ILC1</td><td>T-BET</td><td>IL-12, IL-15, IL-18</td><td>IFN-γ</td><td>Defence against intracellular microbes</td></tr>
<tr><td>ILC2</td><td>GATA3</td><td>IL-25, IL-33, TSLP</td><td>IL-5, IL-13</td><td>Defence against helminths; allergic disease</td></tr>
<tr><td>ILC3</td><td>RORγt</td><td>IL-1, IL-23</td><td>IL-22 and/or IL-17</td><td>Defence against extracellular fungi and bacteria; epithelial barrier</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>NK cells resemble ILC1s because both secrete IFN-γ. However, ILC1s are not cytotoxic. <span class="src" title="M2-F097">Abbas p.82</span></p>
<h3>ILC2s in allergy</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li>Injured or stressed epithelial cells release IL-25, IL-33 and TSLP. IL-33 is an alarmin stored in epithelial nuclei. <span class="src" title="M2-F098">Abbas p.94</span></li>
  <li>These cytokines stimulate ILC2s, Th2 cells and mast cells to make IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13. <span class="src" title="M2-F098">Abbas p.94</span></li>
  <li>ILC2s already sit in the tissue. Their cytokines can start allergic inflammation before Th2 cells arrive. <span class="src" title="M2-F099">Abbas p.465</span></li>
  <li>Later, ILC2s work together with Th2 cells to sustain the inflammation. <span class="src" title="M2-F099">Abbas p.465</span></li>
</ol>
<div class="box analogy"><span class="label">Analogy</span>
<p>ILC2s are the local fire brigade that is already in the building. Th2 cells are the reinforcements that arrive days later with more equipment.</p></div>

<h2 id="ill">8. Innate-like lymphocytes</h2>
<p>Some T and B cells have rearranged antigen receptors of limited diversity. These include invariant natural killer T (iNKT) cells, γδ T cells, mucosa-associated invariant T (MAIT) cells and B-1 cells. They behave more like innate cells than adaptive cells. <span class="src" title="M2-F100">Abbas p.85-86</span></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>MAIT cells</b> recognise bacterial riboflavin metabolites. They make up almost 50% of liver lymphocytes. <span class="src" title="M2-F101">Abbas p.24</span></li>
  <li><b>iNKT cells</b> recognise glycolipids presented by CD1. They rapidly secrete IL-4, IL-10 and IFN-γ. <span class="src" title="M2-F102">Janeway p.119</span></li>
  <li><b>Intraepithelial γδ T cells</b> recognise target molecules directly, not as peptide on MHC. <span class="src" title="M2-F103">Janeway p.119</span></li>
  <li><b>B-1 cells</b> make IgM against polysaccharides within 48 hours, without T-cell help. In mice, they protect against <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>. <span class="src" title="M2-F104">Janeway p.119</span></li>
</ul>

<h2 id="idcards">9. Cell ID cards</h2>
<figure class="diagram" id="D5">
<svg viewBox="0 0 800 530" role="img" aria-labelledby="D5t">
<title id="D5t">ID cards of innate cells: markers, granules or products, main function and key disease for nine cell types</title>
<text x="400" y="26" class="t-title" text-anchor="middle">Innate cell ID cards</text>
<g><rect x="12" y="44" width="250" height="150" rx="8" fill="#f5f7fa" stroke="#2e7d5b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
<rect x="12" y="44" width="250" height="30" rx="8" fill="#2e7d5b"/><rect x="12" y="59" width="250" height="15" fill="#2e7d5b"/>
<text x="22" y="65" class="t-head t-white">Neutrophil</text>
<text x="22" y="96" class="t-body">CD11b, CD18, CD15, CD66b</text>
<text x="22" y="116" class="t-body">Azurophilic: MPO, defensins</text>
<text x="22" y="136" class="t-body">Specific: lysozyme, elastase</text>
